MongoDB collections land in Iceberg as a single `data` JSON string column because OLake flattens JSON keys into a canonical path inside a single JSON column by default, while PostgreSQL tables are structured with explicit schema definitions that OLake can map directly to Iceberg columns [1].

To achieve structured columns for MongoDB data, you can configure normalization in your stream settings. For example, setting `"normalization": true` in your stream configuration will enable level 1 flattening, which transforms nested JSON fields into individual columns [2].

Here is an example of how to configure normalization in your stream settings:

```json
{
  "selected_streams": {
    "namespace": [
      {
        "partition_regex": "",
        "stream_name": "incr",
        "normalization": true,
        "filter": "_id > 6835a56c558d36492e3c39e2 and created_at <= \"2025-05-27T11:43:40.497+00:00\""
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

By enabling normalization, you can ensure that MongoDB collections are structured similarly to PostgreSQL tables in Iceberg.

Yes, the `initial_wait_time: 10` in the MySQL `source.json` is honored by OLake. The `initial_wait_time` is a required parameter under the `update_method` configuration when using CDC, and it specifies the idle timeout for binlog reading [1]. The troubleshooting section also confirms that this value is used during CDC sync to determine how long OLake waits to locate the saved binlog position before timing out [2].

If the value is set to 10, OLake will wait up to 10 seconds for the binlog syncer to locate the saved position. If the search takes longer than this, the syncer will exit due to timeout [2].

Here is an example of the `source.json` configuration with `initial_wait_time: 10`:

```json title="source.json"
{
    "hosts": "mysql-host",
    "username": "mysql-user",
    "password": "mysqlpwd",
    "database": "my-db",
    "port": 3306,
    "tls_skip_verify": true,
    "update_method": {
      "initial_wait_time": 10
    },
    "max_threads": 5,
    "backoff_retry_count": 4,
    "ssh_config": {
      "host": "my-tunnel-host",
      "port": 22,
      "username": "my-tunnel-user",
      "password": "tunnel-password"
    },
    "ssl": {
      "mode": "disable"
    },
    "jdbc_url_params": {
      "connectTimeout": "10000"
    }
}
``` [2]
